WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.475
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.415 --> 00:00:09.420
JIMMY TRAN: Hey there.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:09.970
Jimmy, here.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:11.970
HEATHER CROSS: And hello
again from me, Heather.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.970
On an earlier episode,
we spoke about harnessing

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:16.560
the power of machine learning
with Cloud ML Engine.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.480
Today, we look at how
we can derive insights

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.730
from unstructured text
using Google Cloud Natural

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:22.972
Language, a Google Cloud
Machine Learning API.

00:00:22.972 --> 00:00:24.930
JIMMY TRAN: We'll also
run through a quick demo

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:27.263
of the self-paced lab, where
we'll ask the Cloud Natural

00:00:27.263 --> 00:00:30.454
Language API to extract
entities from a snippet of text.

00:00:30.454 --> 00:00:31.120
Let's get to it.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.109
So I have a question for you.

00:00:38.109 --> 00:00:39.733
How often do you look
at online reviews

00:00:39.733 --> 00:00:40.890
when choosing a restaurant?

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:41.910
HEATHER CROSS: All the
time, especially when

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:43.110
I'm visiting a new city.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:45.630
JIMMY TRAN: So do I. Well, as
valuable as these insights are

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.270
to you as a consumer--
good or bad--

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:51.517
imagine the value to a business
offering a product or service.

00:00:51.517 --> 00:00:52.600
HEATHER CROSS: Absolutely.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.130
From online reviews, social
media posts, blogs, forums,

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.775
and emails, there's so many ways
to connect the written word.

00:00:58.775 --> 00:01:00.900
Think of the amount of
communication data generated

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.462
by call centers as well--

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:04.170
a wealth of
business-critical information

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:05.379
just waiting to be extracted.

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:06.794
JIMMY TRAN: An
example of how this

00:01:06.794 --> 00:01:08.400
can be helpful in
the real world is,

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.470
let's say you're a
business, and you

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.230
need to get real-time
answers to questions like,

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:14.850
are my customers happy?

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.920
What is being said
about our business?

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.657
Or how well is our product
or service being received?

00:01:19.657 --> 00:01:21.990
HEATHER CROSS: This is where
Natural Language Processing

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:24.210
is so valuable, as
it allows machines

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:27.480
to analyze huge volumes of
data that humans never could.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.700
Google Cloud Natural Language
reveals the structure

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.950
and the meaning of text by
offering powerful machine

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:34.950
learning models in an
easy-to-use REST API.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.440
This API brings you the same
machine learning technology

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.740
that powers Google's ability to
find answers in Google search,

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.015
and is the system
behind Google Assistant.

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:50.650
You can use the syntax
and sentiment analysis

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:52.570
functionalities of
Cloud Natural Language

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:55.330
to extract linguistic
information in a block of text,

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:57.577
as well as the overall
feelings expressed with it.

00:01:57.577 --> 00:01:59.410
JIMMY TRAN: You can
also use entity analysis

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:01.790
to return information
about known entities,

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:05.500
such as public figures,
landmarks, organizations,

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.210
products, and so on.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.190
Positive, negative,
or neutral sentiments

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:11.710
can be identified by
combining sentiment analysis

00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:12.640
to these entities too.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.890
HEATHER CROSS: And if you're
in an industry like media

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:16.723
or publishing, where
you would traditionally

00:02:16.723 --> 00:02:19.870
have manually sorted, labeled,
and categorized content,

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:22.480
the Natural Language API
can also automatically sort

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.880
documents and content into more
than 700 predefined categories

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:26.380
for you.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.090
JIMMY TRAN: Time for Qwik labs.

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.790
In this hands-on lab, you'll
use the Cloud Natural Language

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:40.020
API to extract entities like
people, places, and events

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:40.979
from a snippet of text.

00:02:40.979 --> 00:02:42.686
HEATHER CROSS: You
can check out the link

00:02:42.686 --> 00:02:43.990
to start the Qwik lab here.

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:47.720
Keep in mind that this will take
about 40 minutes to complete.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.895
So far in the lab,
we've created an API key

00:02:49.895 --> 00:02:53.180
so that we can use the analyze
entities Natural Language API

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:54.380
method.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:57.450
With this method, the API can
extract entities like people,

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.650
places, and events from text.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.400
We're going to try out
the entity analysis

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:05.120
with the sentence, "Michelangelo
Caravaggio, Italian painter,

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.330
is known for The
Calling of St. Matthew."

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:09.980
First, we're
building our request

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.540
to the Natural Language
API in the request.json

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.820
file in the Cloud Shell editor.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:17.740
We can then pass the
request body along

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:20.110
with the API key environment
variable to the Natural

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:22.540
Language API with
this cURL command.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:26.370
The response shows us the entity
type metadata-- in this case,

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:27.910
a Wikipedia URL--

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:31.420
and [INAUDIBLE]---- the indices
of where the entity appears

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:33.400
in the text from 0 to 1.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.920
And with that, we've sent our
first request to the Cloud

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:37.782
Natural Language API.

00:03:37.782 --> 00:03:40.240
JIMMY TRAN: So that brings us
to an end of another episode.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:41.720
We hope you enjoyed it.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:44.470
If you found some cool ways to
apply the Natural Language API,

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:45.760
we'd love to hear from you.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:46.570
HEATHER CROSS: And
make sure to check out

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:48.236
the link where you
can apply what you've

00:03:48.236 --> 00:03:50.740
learned using the $300 free
trial credit on Google Cloud

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:51.934
Platform here.

00:03:51.934 --> 00:03:54.100
JIMMY TRAN: All of our
additional training resources

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:55.820
are linked below.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:58.107
Thanks for watching
and see you soon.

